      "statement": "<p>Let \\(I\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\) be an interval, \\(f:I\\to\\mathbb R\\), and \\(c\\in I\\). Define \\(q_c:I\\setminus\\{c\\}\\to\\mathbb R\\) by</p>\n\\[q_c(x)=\\frac{f(x)-f(c)}{x-c}.\\]\n<p>We say \\(f\\) is differentiable at \\(c\\) if the limit of \\(q_c\\) at \\(c\\) exists in \\(\\mathbb R\\). Its value is the derivative</p>\n\\[f'(c)=\\lim_{x\\to c}q_c(x).\\]\n<p>If this holds at every \\(c\\in I\\), then \\(f\\) is differentiable on \\(I\\). Its derivative is the function \\(f':I\\to\\mathbb R\\), also denoted by \\(\\mathrm df/\\mathrm dx\\).</p>",
      "idea": "The quotient gives the slope from \\((c,f(c))\\) to \\((x,f(x))\\). At an interior point, \\(f'(c)\\) exists exactly when the left-hand and right-hand limits of this quotient both exist in \\(\\mathbb R\\) and agree. At an included endpoint of a nondegenerate interval, only the limit from within \\(I\\) is used.",
