      "idea": "The Mean Value Theorem transfers a sign from derivatives to secant slopes. Taking a limit transfers the sign of the secant slopes back to the derivative.",
      "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(f'\\ge0\\) on \\(I\\). For any \\(a,b\\in I\\) with \\(a&lt;b\\), the interval \\([a,b]\\) lies in \\(I\\). Differentiability gives continuity on \\([a,b]\\). The Mean Value Theorem gives \\(c\\in(a,b)\\) with \\(f(b)-f(a)=f'(c)(b-a)\\ge0\\), proving that \\(f\\) is increasing.</p>\n<p>Conversely, suppose \\(f\\) is increasing and fix \\(c\\in I\\). For \\(x>c\\), both \\(f(x)-f(c)\\) and \\(x-c\\) are nonnegative. For \\(x&lt;c\\), both are nonpositive. Thus for every \\(x\\in I\\setminus\\{c\\}\\),</p>\n\\[\\frac{f(x)-f(c)}{x-c}\\ge0.\\]\n<p>As \\(x\\to c\\), the quotients converge to \\(f'(c)\\), so \\(f'(c)\\ge0\\). Finally, \\(f\\) is decreasing exactly when \\(-f\\) is increasing. Applying the established equivalence to \\(-f\\) and using \\((-f)'=-f'\\) gives the second equivalence.</p>\n<p>The MVT also gives a strict version. Suppose \\(f\\) is continuous on \\([a,b]\\) and differentiable on \\((a,b).\\) For any \\(a\\le u&lt;v\\le b,\\) there is \\(c\\in(u,v)\\) with \\(f(v)-f(u)=f'(c)(v-u).\\) Thus \\(f'&gt;0\\) throughout \\((a,b)\\) implies that \\(f\\) is strictly increasing on \\([a,b].\\) Likewise \\(f'&lt;0\\) throughout \\((a,b)\\) implies strict decrease.</p>",
